       RRmmtt  provides  remote  access to files and devices for ttaarr(1), ccppiioo(1),
       and similar backup utilities.  It is normally called by running  rrsshh(1)
       or  sssshh(1)  to  the  remote machine, optionally using a different login
       name if one is supplied.

       The calling program communicates with rrmmtt by sending  requests  on  its
       standard input and reading replies from the standard output.  A request
       consists of a request letter followed by an argument (if required)  and
       a  newline character.  Additional data, if any, are sent after the new‐
       line.  On success, rrmmtt returns

           AA_n_u_m_b_e_r\\nn

       where _n_u_m_b_e_r is an ASCII representation of a decimal return code.   Ad‐
       ditional  data  are  returned after this line.  On error, the following
       response is returned:

           EE_e_r_r_n_o\\nn_e_r_r_o_r_-_m_e_s_s_a_g_e\\nn

       where _e_r_r_n_o is one of the system error codes, as described in eerrrrnnoo(3),
       and  _e_r_r_o_r_-_m_e_s_s_a_g_e  is a one-line human-readable description of the er‐
       ror, as printed by ppeerrrroorr(3).

       Available commands and possible responses are discussed  in  detail  in
       the subsequent section.

